--- !u!114 &1831030690
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1831030688}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "<size=100><color=red>Input Explanation</color></size>\r\n\r\nThis section
    of the tutorial explains game input, or how you actually control your robot.\r\nThere
    are two types of input: buttons and camera.\r\n\r\n<color=red>Buttons</color>\r\nButtons
    are a simple and reliable input method. You have five buttons, each corresponding
    to a specific color, with the function written on it.\r\nIf you use default controls,
    the buttons will perform preset functions.\r\nIf you use custom controls (as
    explained in the previous tutorial section), each button will work according
    to the type you assigned it to (If, While, or For).\r\nIf you want precise and
    responsive control, this is the right input method for you.\r\n\r\n<color=red>Camera</color>\r\nCamera
    input is a special and experimental control method. It can be tricky to set up
    and use, but it\u2019s designed for fun, chaotic battles rather than serious
    matches.\r\nInstead of pressing buttons, you control the game by showing objects
    with specific colors to the camera.\r\nHere\u2019s an example of objects used
    for testing:\r\nRed \u2192 Notebook\r\nGreen \u2192 Plant\r\nBlue \u2192 Bottle\r\nWhite
    \u2192 Toilet Paper\r\nBlack \u2192 Cat\r\nThis setup can lead to hilarious gameplay
    moments! However, before the game starts, a test will check if your camera setup
    works correctly. Once you pass the test, you can play normally.\r\nChallenges
    & Tips for Camera Input:\r\nThe biggest issue with camera input is background
    interference.\r\nIf your background contains one of the five colors, the game
    might detect false inputs and activate functions randomly.\r\nTry placing your
    phone in a stable position with a consistent background to avoid unwanted inputs.\r\nOther
    challenges include:\r\nAuto-focus issues \u2013 Many phone cameras adjust focus
    dynamically, which may cause the game to misread colors.\r\nLighting conditions
    \u2013 Some colors may not appear the same under different lighting.\r\nColor
    variations \u2013 Just because something looks green to you doesn\u2019t mean
    the camera will recognize it as green. For example, this text is green, but many
    dark green plants might not be detected correctly.\r\nDifferent camera sensors
    \u2013 A color that works on one phone may not be recognized on another due to
    variations in camera hardware.\r\nHow to Improve Camera Input Accuracy:\r\nAvoid
    standing in front of the camera, as your skin tone or clothing might interfere.\r\nUse
    objects with bright, solid colors that closely match the target color.\r\nExperiment
    with different lighting and environments if the detection isn\u2019t working
    properly.\r\nIf you struggle with camera input, don\u2019t get frustrated, you
    can always switch back to buttons for a reliable experience!"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 32e9087bea9953c46ab22aa5c5c10950, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 9016579006585588636, guid: 32e9087bea9953c46ab22aa5c5c10950, type: 2}
