--- !u!114 &2074738849
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2074738847}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "<size=100><color=red>Parts Explanation</color></size>\r\n\r\nWhen the
    game starts, the first step is to build your robot by selecting the parts you
    like. Below is a complete explanation of parts and their attributes.\r\n\r\nEvery
    robot consists of five different parts: body, melee weapon, ranged weapon, special
    item, and wheels. Each type of part has its own attributes, and every part within
    a type has different attribute values.\r\n\r\n<color=red>After starting the game,
    you will see three options for each type of part, both on the server and on your
    phone. On the server, you can view the attributes and their values, while on
    your phone, you will see part functions. Functions are only relevant if you are
    using custom-programmed controls, if not, you can ignore them.</color>\r\n\r\n<color=red>Body:</color>\r\nThe
    body is the main part of the robot\u2014every other part is attached to it. The
    body contains placeholders for each part, allowing you to see their placement
    and plan an effective setup. For example, if you're using a short-range melee
    weapon, it\u2019s not ideal to pick a body with a middle-placed melee slot, an
    edge placement would be better.\r\nThe body has two attributes:\r\nHealth \u2013
    The total durability of the robot.\r\nArmor \u2013 The percentage of incoming
    damage absorbed.\r\nMore health means less armor, and vice versa.\r\nFunction:\r\nDemolish
    \u2013 Instantly destroys your robot, dealing damage in the process. This can
    be useful when both you and your opponent are low on health, allowing you to
    force a draw.\r\n\r\n<color=red>Melee Weapon:</color>\r\nThe melee weapon is
    your primary damage source in close combat.\r\nAttributes:\r\nAttack Damage \u2013
    The amount of damage per hit.\r\nNumber of Uses \u2013 How many times the weapon
    can be used before cooldown.\r\nCooldown Duration \u2013 The time required before
    the weapon can be used again.\r\nSpeed \u2013 How fast the weapon swings.\r\nFaster
    weapons have more uses but deal less damage per hit.\r\nFunctions:\r\nAttack
    \u2013 Activates the melee weapon.\r\nForce Cooldown \u2013 Manually triggers
    cooldown, allowing you to reset before engaging in combat.\r\n\r\n<color=red>Ranged
    Weapon:</color>\r\nRanged weapons work exactly like melee weapons in terms of
    attributes and functions, except they attack from a distance.\r\n\r\n<color=red>Special
    Item:</color>\r\nSpecial items differ greatly from other parts and from each
    other. Because of this, each special item comes with a description explaining
    its effect.\r\nAttributes:\r\nDescription \u2013 A unique explanation of the
    item\u2019s effect.\r\nUses \u2013 Unlike weapons, special items have a limited
    number of uses with no cooldown. Once all uses are spent, the item is gone for
    the round.\r\nFunction:\r\nUse \u2013 Activates the special item.\r\n\r\n<color=red>Wheels:</color>\r\nWheels
    are responsible for movement.\r\nAttributes:\r\nSpeed \u2013 The higher the value,
    the faster your robot moves.\r\nPower \u2013 Determines how strongly your robot
    pushes against opponents.\r\nPower works differently than you might expect:\r\nA
    more powerful robot can push a weaker one upon collision, potentially throwing
    it off the map to win.\r\nHowever, if a weaker robot has been moving for some
    time, it builds momentum, making it harder to stop. Simply having more power
    doesn\u2019t guarantee an advantage, it depends on the situation.\r\nMore power
    means less speed.\r\nFunctions:\r\nMove Left \u2013 Moves the robot left.\r\nMove
    Right \u2013 Moves the robot right.\r\n"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
